Subject: predicting psi for non-initial seasons?
Message-ID: <GjSXZWCZrAo.2_D7IxidO0wJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/unmarked/c/GjSXZWCZrAo/m/2_D7IxidO0wJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I hadn't seen much of a need for it before, but are alternate
parameterizations possible in colext?  PRESENCE has four; the colext
method (initial psi,gamma,epsilon,p), two seasonal ones (psi for each
season,gamma or epsilon,p), and one other (intial psi, gamma[eps=1-
gamma],p).  I haven't found much helpful info on these
parameterizations (though my Mackenzie book is packed away), but I'm
curious if there is a way in unmarked to predict psi for seasons other
than the first season; e.g., season two in a two-season model.

Along these lines, predictions of gamma (col) and epsilon (ext)
generated by colext appear unconditional on (initial) psi; e.g., a
site with psi = 0.999 can have col = 0.999, likewise, a site with psi
= 0.001 can have ext of =0.999.  This is fine if I want to report a
site colonization probability assuming initial absence or site
extinction probabilities assuming initial presence (which I think is
what is being predicted), but I'm wondering if there are any
calculations that I might make post-hoc to derive what I think of as
'conditional' probabilities of non-initial psi (most important for my
purposes) or, similarly, colonization or extinction probabilities
given the initial occupancy probability.

Thanks for considering the question.

Giancarlo

