Hi Richard,

Thank you for your quick response in clarifying colext probabilities
and for the equation and reference for deriving psi at other values of
t.

I saw in your course code and in code from Royle and Dorazio (http://
www.mbr-pwrc.usgs.gov/pubanalysis/roylebook/panel9pt2.fn.R <http://www.mbr-pwrc.usgs.gov/pubanalysis/roylebook/panel9pt2.fn.R>) an
equation for deriving the probability of 1) turnover and 2)
equilibrium occupancy and I'm wondering if you could elaborate on
these.  I used the turnover equation:

turnover[,t-1] <- (gamma[,t-1] * (1 - psi[,t-1])) / psi[,t]

and found in examining probabilities for psi[t=1] and psi[t=2] that it
corresponded closely to what I would describe as a probability of
colonization given the probability of psi in the two time periods. For
example;

psi[t=1]	psi[t=2]	turnover[t]	gam[t]	eps[t]	phi[t]
0.090	0.845	0.935	0.868	0.387	0.613
0.107	0.937	0.926	0.972	0.352	0.648
0.111	0.957	0.916	0.986	0.278	0.722
0.099	0.891	0.913	0.902	0.214	0.786
0.106	0.935	0.901	0.942	0.124	0.876

(there is only one transition in my data [t1 -> t2] , so I just write
[t])

I always thought 'turnover' was a measure of local extinction. Can you
clarify?

I hadn't heard of the equilibrium occupancy probability you have in
your code:

gamma[,t] / (gamma[,t] + (1 - phi[,t]))

but it didn't appear to correspond to patterns I see in psi[t=1] and
psi[t=2].  Is there a calculation for something corresponding to the
psi-dependent "opposite" of the turnover probability you and Royle &
Dorazio describe?  I apologize that my R & D 2008 is packed away too!

With gratitude,  Giancarlo

