Hi Ian and Andy,

I've been curious about a similar question when interested in
comparing model predictions or parameter estimates from a colext model
assuming p<1 vs. a model that assumes p=1.  Marc Kery et al (2010,
'Predicting species distributions from checklist data using site-
occupancy models') compared single-season occupancy models (p<1) to
binomial GLMs (p=1), but I was hoping to use unmarked to do this in a
multi-season context.

re: offsets in colext, unmarked returns a message that they aren't
supported

I've tried using a method to 'force' p=1 by replacing 0s to 1s in
detection histories that have at least one detection and removing all
detection covariates from models (p~=1).  The models generally 'work',
though occasionally I get Hessian errors.  The one concerning thing
are the SEs for the p intercept (always very large, sometimes NaN),
but psi, gamma, and epsilon parameters are 'normal' (they are similar
to models with p<1) and models produce detection probabilities that
are all equal to 1 (as I hoped).

I would like to use results (predictions, param estimates) from these
'forced perfect detection models', can either of you (or anyone else)
see a problem with using this approach to generating them?

Many thanks as always,

Giancarlo

